{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe one S3-specific failure that causes real data loss is **multiple bundles for one ref**.\nA live experiment this session reproduced it: when two `<sha>.bundle` objects exist for the\n`tickets` ref, `git ls-remote` returns the ref at two SHAs and a fresh clone **hard-fails**\nwith `multiple updates for ref not allowed` — the store becomes unclonable. The push path\nalso stalls: the helper's `remove_remote_ref`/push surface rejects with\n`multiple bundles exists on server`, which `src/rebar/_store/push.py:58` `_is_non_fast_forward`\nclassifies as retriable, but the subsequent fetch cannot resolve the ambiguous ref, so retries\nnever converge.\n\nUpstream ships `git-s3 doctor` (`git_remote_s3.Doctor`), but it is unusable for an autonomous\nfleet, and this was verified against the installed library source (v0.3.2):\n`Doctor.fix_multiple_bundles` is **interactive-only** (two blocking `input()` prompts —\n`manage.py:137,141` — which crash on EOF) and it **discards** the non-chosen head (keeps one\nbundle index, optionally deletes the rest) — i.e. it *loses* every event unique to that head.\nrebar's store is an append-only event log where each divergent head may carry distinct ticket\nevents, so discarding a head is unacceptable.\n\nPrior art for healing a forked append-only log is unanimous (restic `check`/`repair`, git-annex\nunion branch, jj op-log, Fossil/Dolt): **merge, never discard**, and put the storage-specific\nsurface behind a thin seam so the merge logic is written once. rebar already embodies this for\nordinary git remotes: `src/rebar/_store/sync.py` `reconverge()` reconciles divergent\n`tickets`-branch heads by a **lossless multi-parent `git merge`** (both parents kept; conflict\n→ abort → keep local; the only shared mutable file `.bridge_state/*` resolves via the\ntickets-branch `.gitattributes` `merge=ours`; UUID-named event dirs never collide, which is\nexactly why the forbidden `merge=union` driver is unnecessary — see `init.py` `_GITATTRIBUTES`).\n\n**This story therefore reduces the S3 case to the already-solved git case.** The multi-bundle\nstate is ordinary ref divergence *hidden at git-remote-s3's bundle-storage layer*. The only\ngenuinely S3-specific action is unavoidable: a plain fetch+merge+push cannot self-heal because\nthe helper refuses any ref op while `bundles > 1` **before** writing, so rebar must issue at\nleast one S3 delete to collapse the bundle count to ≤1. That delete — plus bundle enumeration\nand the repair lock — are performed **through git-remote-s3's own importable library**, so\nrebar owns no credential chain, key-layout, or lock code, and adds **no** direct S3 SDK\ndependency to core rebar (boto3 ships transitively with the helper, under the optional `[s3]`\nextra only).\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a thin **optional** module `src/rebar/_store/s3_doctor.py` exposing\n`heal_multi_bundle(base_path, remote, ref)`, invoked from `src/rebar/_store/push.py` when the\nmulti-bundle signature is detected, then retry the push once. The module imports\n`git_remote_s3` **lazily inside its functions**, guarded by the existing `require_s3_helper()`\navailability/version check (mirroring `_optional.py`), so importing rebar never requires the\nhelper or boto3.\n\n**Detection (push.py).** Add a narrow `_is_multi_bundle(stderr)` predicate matching the two\nobserved signatures — `multiple bundles exists on server` (helper reject) and\n`multiple updates for ref` (ambiguous fetch/clone). In `push_tickets_branch`'s retry loop, at\nthe existing `if not _is_non_fast_forward(stderr):` seam, branch to the doctor **once** (a\nsingle `healed_once` guard prevents an infinite heal→retry loop) *before* the normal non-FF\nrecovery, then `continue` the loop to re-push.\n\n**Heal algorithm — reuse the helper's S3 primitives + rebar's existing merge; never discard.**\nAll S3 access is via the helper's own `S3Remote` (constructed from the configured `s3://` URL\nwith `parse_git_url`), so credentials/SSE-KMS come from the standard boto3 chain the helper\nalready owns:\n\n1. **Serialize the repair with the helper's own per-ref lock** — call\n   `S3Remote.acquire_lock(ref)` (the helper's `IfNoneMatch=\"*\"` conditional-put with a 60s TTL\n   and stale-lock steal, `remote.py:352`); on `None`/`412` another repair is in flight, so back\n   off and let the normal retry re-detect. `release_lock(ref, lock_key)` in a `finally`. This\n   **reuses** the helper's lock rather than inventing a rebar `DOCTOR` marker.\n2. **Enumerate every head** — `S3Remote.get_bundles_for_ref(ref)` returns the bundle objects\n   for the ref; fetch each bundle's tip into a local scratch ref so all divergent commits are\n   present locally.\n3. **Union-merge losslessly, reusing rebar's existing merge policy** — create a single\n   multi-parent merge commit over all fetched heads with `git merge` under the tickets-branch\n   `.gitattributes` (`.bridge_state/* merge=ours`; UUID event dirs never collide) — the **same\n   lossless semantics `sync._union_merge` already uses**, factored so the doctor and sync share\n   one merge primitive rather than duplicating it. **No `merge=union` driver.** On an\n   unexpected conflict in a non-derived file: abort and surface `rebar fsck-recover` (keep local\n   state), never drop a parent. The merge tip reaches every event from every head.\n4. **Collapse to one bundle** — push the single merged tip as the ref's only bundle via the\n   helper (a normal `git push`, now unambiguous), then delete each superseded old bundle object\n   with `S3Remote.s3.delete_object(Bucket=remote.bucket, Key=...)` (the helper's own boto3\n   client — the one unavoidable S3 delete).\n5. Release the lock.\n\nIdempotency/recovery: the merge tip is content-addressed, so re-running yields the same tip; a\ncrash after a partial delete leaves ≥1 bundle and simply re-heals on the next detection. No\nfencing token is needed — S3 strong read-after-write plus the conditional-put lock make the\nworst case \"multi-bundle again,\" itself healable (Kleppmann: this lock guards *efficiency*, not\ncorrectness). Running the heal lazily on the next push that hits the anomaly (jj's\ndefer-repair-to-next-command / WAL-replay-on-startup) means **no daemon**.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/s3_doctor.py` — NEW optional module: `heal_multi_bundle`, lazy\n  `git_remote_s3` import behind `require_s3_helper()`, the enumerate→fetch→collapse flow, and\n  reuse of the shared merge primitive. No rebar-owned boto3/credential/key-layout/lock code.\n  Target 200–500 LOC; hard cap 800 (split along call-graph seams only).\n- `src/rebar/_store/push.py` — add `_is_multi_bundle`, a `healed_once`-guarded branch to\n  `heal_multi_bundle` at the non-FF seam, and one post-heal push retry.\n- `src/rebar/_store/sync.py` — factor the existing lossless multi-parent merge (currently\n  inside `_union_merge`) into a shared primitive the doctor reuses (behaviour-preserving; sync's\n  own oracle must stay green).\n- `tests/` — new test module (see Testing).\n\n## Testing\n\n- Tier **real temporary git repositories** (git refs / merge / reachability / data loss).\n  Oracle: construct a ref with two divergent bundles, each carrying a **unique** commit; run\n  `heal_multi_bundle` against an **in-process fake `S3Remote`** (models `acquire_lock`/\n  `release_lock`/`get_bundles_for_ref`/`.s3.delete_object` over a local object dir, so no real\n  AWS and no new hard test dep); assert (a) exactly one bundle remains for the ref, (b) a fresh\n  clone succeeds, and (c) **every** unique commit from **both** heads is reachable from the\n  healed tip (collateral invariant: no unique commit dropped) — the exact loss upstream's\n  discard would cause.\n- Tier **multi-process locking race** (repair storm). Oracle: N real processes hit the\n  multi-bundle state behind a shared barrier against the shared fake; assert exactly one\n  performs the merge (others get `None` from `acquire_lock` and back off) and the final state is\n  a single healed bundle with full reachability.\n- Tier **crash-recovery** (kill after failpoint). Oracle: inject a failure between \"push merged\n  bundle\" and \"delete old bundles\"; on the next detection the doctor re-heals to a single bundle\n  with no loss (idempotency).\n- Tier **push-path integration**. Oracle: seed the `multiple bundles` stderr signature and\n  assert `push_tickets_branch` auto-invokes the heal exactly once and then converges (no\n  infinite non-FF retry loop).\n- **Opt-in live canary** (`tests/e2e/test_s3_doctor_live.py`, AWS-credential-gated, skipped in\n  credential-less CI) exercises the happy path against a real ephemeral bucket to validate the\n  real conditional-write/multi-bundle semantics the fake models.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] After `heal_multi_bundle` on a ref with two divergent bundles, exactly one bundle remains,\n      a fresh clone succeeds, and every unique commit from both heads is reachable from the\n      healed tip. Verified by the real-git-repo oracle.\n- [ ] The heal never discards a head/event: a commit unique to the non-chosen head is present in\n      the healed history (the specific regression upstream's interactive doctor causes). Verified\n      by the reachability assertion.\n- [ ] Concurrent doctors do not corrupt or duplicate-repair: with N processes behind a barrier,\n      exactly one merges (the rest observe the helper's held per-ref lock and back off) and the\n      final state is a single healed bundle. Verified by the multi-process race oracle.\n- [ ] The heal is idempotent under a mid-repair crash: a failure between push-merged and\n      delete-old re-heals to a single bundle with no loss on the next run. Verified by the\n      crash-recovery oracle.\n- [ ] `push_tickets_branch` auto-invokes the heal on the multi-bundle signature exactly once and\n      the push converges afterward (no infinite non-FF retry loop). Verified by the push-path\n      test.\n- [ ] The heal keeps append-only semantics and adds **no** direct S3 SDK dependency to core\n      rebar: all S3 access (lock, enumerate, delete) is via git-remote-s3's own `S3Remote`\n      imported lazily behind `require_s3_helper()`; no `merge=union` driver is used; the helper\n      lock TTL is unchanged (default 60s). Verified by inspection and by rebar importing with the\n      `[s3]` extra absent.","title":"Non-interactive auto-doctor: heal S3 multiple-bundles by lossless merge (reuse helper lib + rebar reconcile; never discard)"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786209684836158001,"uuid":"6af750f5-f010-4a57-998c-99fc00948744"}